    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nHyundai Shelves the N Vision 74 Indefinitely\n12 photos\nPhoto: Hyundai\nThe Hyundai N Vision 74 was confirmed for production in August 2024, and we were all waiting to see the firm’s first real supercar. However, it turns out that we have been waiting in vain, because Hyundai shelved the radical project indefinitely.\nHyundai has officially shelved production plans for the N Vision 74 , confirming that the retro-futuristic hydrogen coupe will not be built for the foreseeable future. Hyundai Design Center chief Simon Loasby revealed that the automaker simply could not make the financial math work.\nLoasby explained that evaluating the car solely on its expected sales volume and direct impact left it with \"no chance\" whatsoever of turning a profit. Even when factoring in its potential as a halo car, the financial case still fell short.\nThe decision comes after Hyundai invested serious effort and money into the project following its debut back in July 2022 as a high-performance \"rolling lab\" concept and its confirmation for production in August 2024. Hyundai filed trademarks for the model in August 2026. The automaker has reportedly built several prototypes and completed a full-production-ready clay model of both the exterior and interior.\nPhoto: Hyundai However, something happened along the way, and the executives decided to pull the plug on what might have been Hyundai’s first production supercar. Speaking to The Drive at a media preview for the launch of the 2027 Hyundai Elantra , Simon Loasby revealed that the model slated for production before the end of the decade is no longer coming.\nThe N Vision 74 was supposed to hit the market powered by two electric motors, mounted out back, and featuring a hydrogen fuel cell and a battery with a capacity of 62.4 kWh. Hyundai claimed the car could drive as far as 373 miles (600 kilometers)l.\nSitting on an 800-volt architecture, the dual-motor powertrain produced 671 horsepower (680 metric horsepower) and 664 pound-feet (900 Newton meters) of torque, which would have pushed it from 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) in under 4.0 seconds and on to a top speed of 155 mph (250 kph).\nThe N Vision 74 may be shelved, but Hyundai has big plans for the near future. The first step was the official unveiling of the new-generation Elantra both as a pure ICE with the 2.0-liter inline-four, coming next year, and as a hybrid with the 1.6-liter four-cylinder engine working alongside an electric motor, ready to hit the market in 2028. An N variant is expected to drop in the lineup at a later date.\nPhoto: Hyundai As for the N Vision 74 supercar, Loasby left the door cracked, saying, \" At the moment, no ,\" in a conversaton with The Drive. That sounds like the model might go into production when the market conditions are kinder to hydrogen-powered cars.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHyundai N Vision 74 Hyundai Hyundai supercar Hyundai Elantra canceled Hyundai\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Black Friday for Riders: Take a Look at a Few Brands With Massive Sales on Their Phone Mounts",
